{
  "table": "Table 13. Maximum depth gradient and inversion factors",
  "source": "World Ocean Database 2023 User's Manual (knowledge/wod_readme.pdf)",
  "page": 56,
  "definitions": {
    "gradient": "(v2 - v1) / (z2 - z1), where 1 is the current level and 2 the next level (Equation 1, p.53)",
    "MGV": "Maximum Gradient Value, for a negative gradient (an excessive decrease with depth). Exceeding it sets observed-level flag 3.",
    "MIV": "Maximum Inversion Value, for a positive gradient (an excessive increase with depth). Exceeding it sets observed-level flag 2.",
    "ZSI": "Zero Sensitivity Indicator. Flags a value of zero as a missing-value marker when the gradient decreasing to zero exceeds MGV * ZSI. Sets flag 4."
  },
  "rules": [
    "MGV and MIV are separate thresholds, not one symmetric threshold.",
    "Separate values apply above and below 400 m depth.",
    "For high-resolution instruments (HCTD, XBT) use a minimum depth difference of 3.0 m when computing the gradient.",
    "Salinity MGV/MIV above 400 m are deliberately huge and near-irrelevant to the check."
  ],
